<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Final exam problems on lines and planes in space
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-lines-planes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-lines-planes" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These final exam problems intersect lines with planes, build a plane from a line, and measure the distance between two lines. If you would like to review this topic first, see <xref ref="chapter-lines-planes" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rf-4">
<statement>
<p>
Find the vector equation of the line of intersection of the following
planes:
<md>
<mrow>2x + y - z \amp = 6,</mrow>
<mrow>x - y + 3z \amp = 4.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The normal vectors are <m>\overrightarrow{n}_1 = \langle 2, 1, -1\rangle</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_2 = \langle 1, -1, 3\rangle</m>, so the direction vector of
the line of intersection is
<md>
\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{n}_1 \times \overrightarrow{n}_2 =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 3
\end{vmatrix}
= \langle 2, -7, -3\rangle.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Next we need a point on the line. Setting <m>x = 0</m> in the
equations of the planes gives
<md>
<mrow>y - z \amp = 6</mrow>
<mrow>-y + 3z \amp = 4</mrow>
</md>
Adding the two equations gives <m>2z = 10</m>, so <m>z = 5</m> and
<m>y = 11</m>. Hence the point <m>(0, 11, 5)</m> is on the line, and
the vector equation of the line of intersection is
<md>
\mathbf r(t) = \langle 0, 11, 5\rangle + t\,\langle 2, -7, -3\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-6">
<statement>
<p>
Find the intersection of the line
<m>\mathbf r(t) = \langle 1, 0, 1\rangle + t\langle -1, 1, 1\rangle</m>
and the plane <m>2x - y + z = 1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The parametric equations of the line are
<md>
x(t) = 1 - t, \qquad y(t) = t, \qquad z(t) = 1 + t.
</md>
Substituting into the equation of the plane,
<md>
2x - y + z = 2(1-t) - t + (1+t) = 3 - 2t = 1
\;\Rightarrow\; t = 1,
</md>
and <m>x(1) = 0</m>, <m>y(1) = 1</m>, <m>z(1) = 2</m>. The
intersection is the point <m>(0, 1, 2)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-5">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation of a plane containing the line
<m>\mathbf r = \langle -4, 5, -1\rangle + t\langle -4, 6, -3\rangle</m>
and parallel to the plane <m>-3x - y + 2z = 0</m>, in which the
coefficient of <m>x</m> is <m>-3</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Since the desired plane is parallel to <m>-3x - y + 2z = 0</m>, it has
the same normal vector
<md>
\overrightarrow{n} = \langle -3, -1, 2\rangle = \langle A, B, C\rangle.
</md>
(Note that <m>\overrightarrow{n} \cdot \langle -4, 6, -3\rangle
= 12 - 6 - 6 = 0</m>, so the line is indeed parallel to such a plane.)
When <m>t = 0</m>, the point <m>(-4, 5, -1)</m> is on the plane, so
from the point-normal form,
<md>
<mrow>A(x - x_0) + B(y - y_0) + C(z - z_0) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad -3(x+4) - (y-5) + 2(z+1) \amp = 0.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-5">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the distance between the following two lines:
<md>
<mrow>L_1: \amp \quad x = 4 - t, \quad y = 3 + 2t, \quad z = -5 + 3t</mrow>
<mrow>L_2: \amp \quad x = 5 - t, \quad y = 3 + 2t, \quad z = -5 + 3t</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We notice that the two lines are parallel (both have direction vector
<m>\mathbf v = \langle -1, 2, 3\rangle</m>) and therefore we can find
the distance between a point on <m>L_2</m> and the line <m>L_1</m>.
Taking <m>S(5, 3, -5)</m> on <m>L_2</m> and <m>P(4, 3, -5)</m> on
<m>L_1</m>, we get <m>\overrightarrow{PS} = \langle 1, 0, 0\rangle</m>, and
<m>\|\mathbf v\| = \sqrt{1 + 4 + 9} = \sqrt{14}</m>.
</p>

<p>
Using the cross product,
<md>
\overrightarrow{PS} \times \mathbf v =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
-1 \amp 2 \amp 3
\end{vmatrix}
= -3\,\hat{\jmath} + 2\,\hat{k},
\qquad
\left\|\overrightarrow{PS} \times \mathbf v\right\| = \sqrt{9 + 4} = \sqrt{13},
</md>
and the distance is
<md>
d = \frac{\left\|\overrightarrow{PS} \times \mathbf v\right\|}{\|\mathbf v\|}
= \frac{\sqrt{13}}{\sqrt{14}}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-5">
<statement>
<p>
Find the distance between the two planes
<md>
x + 2y + z = 1
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
2x + 4y + 2z = 7.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Dividing the second equation by <m>2</m> gives
<m>x + 2y + z = \frac72</m>, so both planes have the same normal
vector <m>\overrightarrow{n} = \langle 1, 2, 1\rangle</m> and are therefore
<em>parallel</em>. (They are distinct, since <m>1 \ne \frac72</m>.)
</p>

<p>
Pick any point on the first plane, say <m>P(1,0,0)</m>, and any point
on the second, say <m>S\left(\frac72, 0, 0\right)</m>. Then
<m>\overrightarrow{PS} = \left\langle \frac52, 0, 0\right\rangle</m>, and the
distance between the planes is the length of the projection of
<m>\overrightarrow{PS}</m> onto <m>\overrightarrow{n}</m>:
<md>
d = \frac{\left|\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}\right|}{\|\overrightarrow{n}\|}
= \frac{\left|\frac52 \cdot 1 + 0 + 0\right|}{\sqrt{1+4+1}}
= \frac{5}{2\sqrt6}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-12">
<statement>
<p>
Find the angle between the planes <m>2x + 2y = 7</m> and
<m>y + z = -1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The angle between two planes equals the angle between their normal
vectors, <m>\overrightarrow{n}_1 = \langle 2, 2, 0\rangle</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_2 = \langle 0, 1, 1\rangle</m>:
<md>
\cos\theta
= \frac{\overrightarrow{n}_1 \cdot \overrightarrow{n}_2}{\|\overrightarrow{n}_1\|\,\|\overrightarrow{n}_2\|}
= \frac{0 + 2 + 0}{\sqrt8 \cdot \sqrt2}
= \frac{2}{4}
= \frac12,
</md>
so <m>\theta = \dfrac{\pi}{3}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-14">
<statement>
<p>
There is a line <m>L</m> parametrized by <m>x = 1 + 2t</m>,
<m>y = 3 + 4t</m>, <m>z = 5 + 6t</m>. The line passes through some
point <m>P</m> whose <m>z</m>-coordinate is <m>-7</m>. Find an
equation for the plane through <m>P</m> normal to <m>L</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First locate <m>P</m>. Setting <m>z = -7</m>,
<md>
5 + 6t = -7 \;\Rightarrow\; t = -2,
</md>
so <m>x = 1 + 2(-2) = -3</m> and <m>y = 3 + 4(-2) = -5</m>, i.e.
<m>P(-3, -5, -7)</m>.
</p>

<p>
A plane normal to <m>L</m> has the direction vector of <m>L</m> as its
normal vector, <m>\overrightarrow{n} = \langle 2, 4, 6\rangle</m>. Therefore the
plane is
<md>
2(x+3) + 4(y+5) + 6(z+7) = 0.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-17">
<statement>
<p>
Find parametric equations for the line through <m>(2,1,1)</m> which is
normal to the plane containing <m>(0,1,1)</m>, <m>(-1,2,4)</m> and
<m>(3,0,1)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
From <xref ref="pp-13"/>, the plane through those three points has
normal vector <m>\overrightarrow{n} = \langle 3, 9, -2\rangle</m>. A line normal
to the plane has <m>\overrightarrow{n}</m> as its direction vector, so through
<m>(2,1,1)</m>,
<md>
x = 2 + 3t, \qquad y = 1 + 9t, \qquad z = 1 - 2t,
\qquad t \in \R.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2a-4">
<statement>
<p>
A surveyor determines that the three points
<md>
A = (-1,2,16), \qquad B = (2,-2,17), \qquad C = (2,0,16)
</md>
all lie on a flat surface <m>W</m>.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Find the point of <m>W</m> that is closest to the point
<m>(4,4,-3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find parametric equations for a line that passes through
<m>(4,4,-3)</m>, never crosses <m>W</m>, and satisfies
<m>x(t) = y(t)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>Finding the plane.</term> Two vectors lying in <m>W</m> are
<md>
\overrightarrow{AB} = \left\langle 3,\ -4,\ 1 \right\rangle ,
\qquad
\overrightarrow{AC} = \left\langle 3,\ -2,\ 0 \right\rangle .
</md>
A normal vector is their cross product:
<md>
<mrow>\overrightarrow{n} \amp = \overrightarrow{AB}\times\overrightarrow{AC}
= \begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
3 \amp -4 \amp 1 \\
3 \amp -2 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle (-4)(0)-(1)(-2),\ (1)(3)-(3)(0),\ (3)(-2)-(-4)(3)
\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle 2,\ 3,\ 6 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
Note <m>\|\overrightarrow{n}\| = \sqrt{4+9+36} = 7</m>, which will be convenient.
Using the point <m>A</m>, the plane is
<md>
<mrow>2(x+1) + 3(y-2) + 6(z-16) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>2x + 3y + 6z \amp = 100 .</mrow>
</md>
(Check: <m>B</m> gives <m>4-6+102 = 100</m> and <m>C</m> gives
<m>4+0+96 = 100</m>.)
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> The closest point of a plane to a given point
<m>Q = (4,4,-3)</m> is the foot of the perpendicular from <m>Q</m>,
which we reach by moving from <m>Q</m> in the direction of the normal.
So consider
<md>
\overrightarrow{r}(t) = Q + t\,\overrightarrow{n} = (4+2t,\ 4+3t,\ -3+6t)
</md>
and find the value of <m>t</m> for which this point lies on <m>W</m>:
<md>
<mrow>2(4+2t) + 3(4+3t) + 6(-3+6t) \amp = 100</mrow>
<mrow>8 + 4t + 12 + 9t - 18 + 36t \amp = 100</mrow>
<mrow>2 + 49t \amp = 100</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp = 2 .</mrow>
</md>
Therefore the closest point is
<md>
\overrightarrow{r}(2) = (4+4,\ 4+6,\ -3+12) = (8,\ 10,\ 9) .
</md>
(Check: <m>16 + 30 + 54 = 100</m>.) Incidentally, the
distance from <m>Q</m> to <m>W</m> is
<m>|t|\,\|\overrightarrow{n}\| = 2\cdot 7 = 14</m>.
See <xref ref="fig-m2a-plane"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2a-plane">
<caption>
Schematic picture of <xref ref="m2a-4"/>. From <m>Q(4,4,-3)</m> we
travel along the normal direction <m>\overrightarrow{n} = \langle 2,3,6\rangle</m>
until we hit <m>W</m>, arriving at <m>(8,10,9)</m>. Any line through
<m>Q</m> whose direction is perpendicular to <m>\overrightarrow{n}</m> stays
parallel to <m>W</m> and never meets it.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A tilted plane W drawn as a parallelogram, with the point Q below it.
A normal arrow runs from Q up to the foot of the perpendicular on the
plane, and a second line through Q is drawn parallel to the plane,
never meeting it.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
% the plane W as a parallelogram
\fill[mblue, opacity=0.20] (-3.2,0.9) -- (2.6,2.1) -- (4.4,0.9)
      -- (-1.4,-0.3) -- cycle;
\draw[mblue, thick] (-3.2,0.9) -- (2.6,2.1) -- (4.4,0.9)
      -- (-1.4,-0.3) -- cycle;
\node[mblue] at (-2.75,1.45) {$W$};
% the point Q and the foot F
\coordinate (Q) at (0.2,-2.5);
\coordinate (F) at (1.15,0.85);
\draw[->, very thick, mverm] (Q) -- (F)
      node[midway, right] {$\overrightarrow{n}=\langle 2,3,6\rangle$};
\fill[mred] (Q) circle (2pt);
\fill[mverm] (F) circle (2pt);
\node[mred, anchor=north] at (Q) {$Q(4,4,-3)$};
\node[mverm, anchor=south west] at (F) {$(8,10,9)$};
% the parallel line through Q
\draw[very thick, mgreen] (-3.0,-2.94) -- (3.6,-2.06);
\node[mgreen, anchor=north east] at (-1.3,-2.98)
      {$\ell:\ \overrightarrow{d}=\langle 6,6,-5\rangle$};
% right angle mark at F
\draw[mgray] ($(F)+(-0.20,-0.18)$) -- ($(F)+(-0.02,-0.30)$)
             -- ($(F)+(0.10,-0.12)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> A line never crosses the plane <m>W</m> exactly when
it is parallel to <m>W</m> and does not lie in it. Since
<m>Q = (4,4,-3)</m> is not on <m>W</m> (we saw
<m>2(4)+3(4)+6(-3) = 2 \ne 100</m>), we only need the direction vector
<m>\overrightarrow{d} = \left\langle a, b, c\right\rangle</m> to be perpendicular
to the normal:
<md>
\overrightarrow{d} \cdot \overrightarrow{n} = 2a + 3b + 6c = 0 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
The extra requirement <m>x(t) = y(t)</m> forces the two lines
<m>x = 4 + at</m> and <m>y = 4 + bt</m> to agree for all <m>t</m>,
which happens exactly when <m>a = b</m>. Substituting <m>b = a</m>,
<md>
2a + 3a + 6c = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad 5a = -6c .
</md>
Choosing <m>a = 6</m> and <m>c = -5</m> gives
<m>\overrightarrow{d} = \left\langle 6,\ 6,\ -5 \right\rangle</m>, and the line is
<md>
x = 4 + 6t, \qquad y = 4 + 6t, \qquad z = -3 - 5t .
</md>
Indeed <m>x(t) = y(t)</m> for every <m>t</m>, and
<m>\overrightarrow{d}\cdot\overrightarrow{n} = 12+18-30 = 0</m>, so the line is parallel to
<m>W</m> and, starting from a point off <m>W</m>, never reaches it.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-3">
<statement>
<p>
The parametric equations
<md>
<mrow>x \amp = t+1,</mrow>
<mrow>y \amp = 2t, \qquad -\infty \lt t \lt \infty</mrow>
<mrow>z \amp = 2 + 3t - t^2,</mrow>
</md>
sweep out a parabola lying in some plane in <m>xyz</m>-space. Find the
equation of that plane.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The cleanest way to see the plane is to write the position vector as a
constant vector plus multiples of two fixed vectors:
<md>
\overrightarrow{r}(t) = \left\langle t+1,\ 2t,\ 2+3t-t^2 \right\rangle
= \left\langle 1,0,2 \right\rangle
+ t\left\langle 1,\ 2,\ 3 \right\rangle
+ t^2\left\langle 0,\ 0,\ -1 \right\rangle .
</md>
Whatever <m>t</m> is, the point <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t)</m> is the fixed point
<m>(1,0,2)</m> plus a combination of the two fixed vectors
<m>\overrightarrow{u} = \left\langle 1,2,3\right\rangle</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{w} = \left\langle 0,0,-1\right\rangle</m>. So the whole curve
lies in the plane through <m>(1,0,2)</m> spanned by <m>\overrightarrow{u}</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{w}</m>.
</p>

<p>
A normal vector to that plane is
<md>
<mrow>\overrightarrow{n} \amp = \overrightarrow{u} \times \overrightarrow{w}
= \begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp -1
\end{vmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle (2)(-1)-(3)(0),\ (3)(0)-(1)(-1),\ 0-0 \right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle -2,\ 1,\ 0 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Using the point <m>(1,0,2)</m>, the plane is
<md>
<mrow>-2(x-1) + 1(y-0) + 0(z-2) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>-2x + y + 2 \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>2x - y \amp = 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
There is a quicker route to the same answer once you notice that the
normal has no <m>\hat{k}</m>-component: the plane is vertical, so its
equation involves only <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Eliminating <m>t</m> from
the first two equations, <m>t = x - 1</m> and <m>t = y/2</m>, gives
<md>
x - 1 = \frac{y}{2} \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad 2x - y = 2 ,
</md>
and this relation holds for every <m>t</m> regardless of the value of
<m>z</m>. The curve is a parabola drawn on this vertical wall; see
<xref ref="fig-m2b-parabola"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2b-parabola">
<caption>
The curve <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t) = \left\langle t+1,\ 2t,\ 2+3t-t^2
\right\rangle</m> is a parabola lying in the vertical plane
<m>2x - y = 2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A vertical plane drawn as a tilted rectangle in three dimensional
space, with a downward opening parabola drawn on it.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(0.62cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,0.62cm)}]
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (-1.2,0,0) -- (5.2,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-4.5,0) -- (0,8.5,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,-6.5) -- (0,0,6.5) node[above] {$z$};
% the plane 2x - y = 2, parametrized as (1+s, 2s, w)
\fill[mblue, opacity=0.16]
   (-0.6,-3.2,-6.0) -- (3.4,4.8,-6.0) -- (3.4,4.8,5.8)
   -- (-0.6,-3.2,5.8) -- cycle;
\draw[mblue, thick]
   (-0.6,-3.2,-6.0) -- (3.4,4.8,-6.0) -- (3.4,4.8,5.8)
   -- (-0.6,-3.2,5.8) -- cycle;
\node[mblue, anchor=north east] at (-0.6,-3.2,-5.6) {$2x-y=2$\;};
% the parabola
\draw[very thick, mverm, smooth, samples=70, domain=-1.6:3.6,
      variable=\t]
   plot ({\t+1},{2*\t},{2+3*\t-\t*\t});
% vertex of the parabola at t = 1.5
\fill[mred] (2.5,3,4.25) circle (1.7pt);
\node[mred, anchor=south west, fill=white, fill opacity=0.8,
      text opacity=1, inner sep=1pt] at (2.5,3,4.35)
  {$\left(\tfrac52,3,\tfrac{17}{4}\right)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-5">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>L</m> be the line given by
<md>
x = 4+t, \qquad y = -1-2t, \qquad z = 5+t .
</md>
Find the point on <m>L</m> that is closest to <m>(-2,2,-1)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Write <m>Q = (-2,2,-1)</m> and let
<m>P(t) = (4+t,\ -1-2t,\ 5+t)</m> be the general point of <m>L</m>. The
direction vector of the line is
<m>\overrightarrow{d} = \left\langle 1,\ -2,\ 1\right\rangle</m>.
</p>

<p>
The closest point is the foot of the perpendicular from <m>Q</m>, so we
want the value of <m>t</m> for which <m>\overrightarrow{QP}</m> is orthogonal to
<m>\overrightarrow{d}</m>. First,
<md>
<mrow>\overrightarrow{QP} \amp = \left\langle (4+t)-(-2),\ (-1-2t)-2,\ (5+t)-(-1)
\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle 6+t,\ -3-2t,\ 6+t \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now impose <m>\overrightarrow{QP}\cdot\overrightarrow{d} = 0</m>:
<md>
<mrow>(6+t)(1) + (-3-2t)(-2) + (6+t)(1) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>6 + t + 6 + 4t + 6 + t \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>18 + 6t \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp = -3 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Substituting <m>t = -3</m> into the parametric equations gives the
closest point
<md>
P(-3) = \left(4-3,\ -1+6,\ 5-3\right) = (1,\ 5,\ 2) .
</md>
As a check, at <m>t=-3</m> we get
<m>\overrightarrow{QP} = \left\langle 3,\ 3,\ 3 \right\rangle</m>, and
<md>
\overrightarrow{QP}\cdot\overrightarrow{d}
 = \left\langle 3,3,3\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 1,-2,1\right\rangle
 = 3 - 6 + 3 = 0 ,
</md>
as required. The distance from <m>Q</m> to the line is
<m>\|\overrightarrow{QP}\| = \sqrt{9+9+9} = 3\sqrt3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
